layout: lr
crossings: 4
title: "Kubernetes request path"
subtitle: "kubectl apply through running pod — control plane decides, kubelet executes"
caption: "Source: examples/39-kubernetes-request-path.melk"
legend: on

# How `kubectl apply` becomes a running pod:
#
#   1. kubectl POSTs the desired state to kube-apiserver.
#   2. The API server authenticates and authorises (RBAC).
#   3. State is persisted to etcd — every read and write goes
#      through it; it's the single source of truth.
#   4. The scheduler watches for unscheduled pods and binds them
#      to a node, writing the binding back through the API server.
#   5. The kubelet on the target node reconciles its assignments
#      and asks the container runtime to start the container.
#
# Spine reads as the request flow; etcd sits above as the state
# store; scheduler sits below as the watch-loop client driving
# placement. Both side-channels close back through the API server
# in reality (watch loops); the diagram shows the dominant direction.
#
# `etcd` is tagged `critical` because losing it loses the cluster.

kubectl     { shape: rect,      label: "kubectl\n(apply)" }
apiserver   { shape: rect,      label: "kube-\napiserver" }
auth        { shape: diamond,   label: "authn\n+ RBAC" }
kubelet     { shape: rect,      label: "kubelet" }
runtime     { shape: rect,      label: "container\nruntime" }
pod         { shape: roundrect, label: "pod" }

etcd        { shape: cylinder,  label: "etcd", tags: [critical] }
scheduler   { shape: roundrect, label: "kube-\nscheduler" }

# Control + data plane spine.
pipeline request: kubectl -> apiserver -> auth -> kubelet -> runtime -> pod

# State store sits above the apiserver (default :left in LR = up).
branch state-store: apiserver -> etcd

# Scheduler sits below auth (`:right` in LR = down). It receives
# unscheduled pods after RBAC has cleared them.
branch scheduling:right: auth -> scheduler
